# The VPS's own `.env`, next to the compose files. NOT the same file as
# `backend/.env` -- that one is read by the container and holds the application's
# secrets; this one is read by `docker compose` itself, before any container
# exists, to fill in `${...}` in docker-compose.prod.yml.
#
# Copy to `/opt/ai_chatbot/.env` on the VPS. Nothing in it is a secret -- unlike
# backend/.env, which is why this example can carry real values rather than blanks.
# The `.env` itself stays out of the repository so that a checkout on a laptop
# does not silently start resolving compose variables to the VPS's answers.

# Which compose file a bare `docker compose ...` uses in this directory.
#
# This line exists because of an outage on 2026-09-13. `docker compose up -d
# --force-recreate backend`, run in /opt/ai_chatbot without `-f`, picked up
# docker-compose.yml -- the LOCAL DEVELOPMENT file. That one brings its own MySQL
# and Redis, builds the backend from source, and publishes 127.0.0.1:8000. Compose
# matched the running production containers by project and service name, tore the
# backend down, and could not start the replacement because another project on the
# box already had port 8000. The site served 502 until it was put back by hand.
#
# With this set, a bare `docker compose` in this directory means the production
# file. The deploy workflow's explicit `-f docker-compose.prod.yml` still wins, so
# nothing about the deploy changes.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.prod.yml

# The images the deploy workflow builds and pushes. `docker-compose.prod.yml`
# substitutes them, so compose fails outright if they are missing -- which is the
# right failure, since the alternative is an `image:` line that resolves to
# nothing.
BACKEND_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kelvinpang90/ai_chatbot_demo-backend
FRONTEND_IMAGE=ghcr.io/kelvinpang90/ai_chatbot_demo-frontend
